"The Merino–Welsh conjecture is false for matroids (https://real.mtak.hu/193338/1/1-s2.0-S0001870824001890-main.pdf)\nciteturn1search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.5 years ago; Tutte polynomial and Merino–Welsh conjecture\n2  \nC. Beke et al. / Advances in Mathematics 446 (2024) 109674\n\nTutte polynomial and Merino–Welsh conjecture\n\n\\(T_M(2,0)T_M(0,2) \\ge T_M(1,1)^2.\\)\n\nWe show a counter-example for these inequalities.  \n© 2024 The Authors. Published by Elsevier Inc. This is an open access article under the CC BY-NC-ND license  \n(http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/).\n\n# 1. Introduction\n\nFor a connected graph \\(G\\), let \\(\\tau(G)\\), \\(\\alpha(G)\\) and \\(\\alpha^*(G)\\) denote the number of spanning trees, the number of acyclic orientations and the number of strongly connected orientations, respectively. Merino and Welsh [18] conjectured that if \\(G\\) is a connected graph without loops and bridges, then\n\n\\[\n\\max(\\alpha(G), \\alpha^*(G)) \\ge \\tau(G).\n\\]\n\nNote that \\(\\alpha(G)\\), \\(\\alpha^*(G)\\), and \\(\\tau(G)\\) are all evaluations of the Tutte polynomial, namely,  \n\\(T_G(2,0)=\\alpha(G)\\), \\(T_G(0,2)=\\alpha^*(G)\\), and \\(T_G(1,1)=\\tau(G)\\), where the Tutte polynomial \\(T_G(x,y)\\) is defined as\n\n\\[\nT_G(x,y)=\\sum_{A\\subseteq E}(x-1)^{k(A)-k(E)}(y-1)^{k(A)+|A|-v(G)},\n\\]\n\nwith \\(k(A)\\) denoting the number of connected components of the graph \\((V,A)\\), see [22]. There is a vast amount of literature on the properties of the Tutte polynomial and its applications, for instance, [2,5,6,23], or the book [7].\n\nConde and Merino [4] also suggested the following “additive” and “multiplicative” versions of the conjecture:\n\n\\[\nT_G(2,0)+T_G(0,2)\\ge 2T_G(1,1),\n\\]\n\nand\n\n\\[\nT_G(2,0)T_G(0,2)\\ge T_G(1,1)^2.\n\\]\n\nIt is easy to see that the multiplicative version implies the additive version which in turn implies the maximum version.\n\nThe Merino–Welsh conjecture and its variants triggered considerable attention. Thomassen [21] proved that the conjecture is true if the graph \\(G\\) is sufficiently sparse or sufficiently dense. Lin [15] proved it for 3-connected graphs satisfying certain degree conditions. Noble and Royle [19] proved the multiplicative version for series-parallel graphs.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Merino--Welsh conjecture is false for matroids (https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2311.01932)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.0 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; View a PDF of the paper titled The Merino--Welsh conjecture is false for matroids, by Csongor Beke and 3 other authors\n\n# Mathematics > Combinatorics\n\n[Submitted on 3 Nov 2023 (v1), last revised 23 Feb 2024 (this version, v2)]\n\n# Title:The Merino--Welsh conjecture is false for matroids\n\nAuthors:Csongor Beke, Gergely Kál Csáji, Péter Csikvári, Sára Pituk\n\nView a PDF of the paper titled The Merino--Welsh conjecture is false for matroids, by Csongor Beke and 3 other authors\n\nView PDF HTML (experimental)\n\n> Abstract:The matroidal version of the Merino--Welsh conjecture states that the Tutte polynomial T M(x,y)$T_M(x,y)$ of any matroid M$M$ without loops and coloops satisfies that\n> \n> max(T M(2,0),T M(0,2))≥T M(1,1).\n> \n> $$\\max(T_M(2,0),T_M(0,2))\\geq T_M(1,1).$$ Equivalently, if the Merino--Welsh conjecture is true for all matroids without loops and coloops, then the following inequalities are also satisfied for all matroids without loops and coloops:\n> \n> T M(2,0)+T M(0,2)≥2 T M(1,1),\n> \n> $$T_M(2,0)+T_M(0,2)\\geq 2T_M(1,1),$$ and\n> \n> T M(2,0)T M(0,2)≥T M(1,1)2.\n> \n> $$T_M(2,0)T_M(0,2)\\geq T_M(1,1)^2.$$ We show a counter-example for these inequalities.\n\nComments:  | 6 pages\nSubjects:  | Combinatorics (math.CO)\nCite as:  | arXiv:2311.01932 [math.CO]\n   | (or arXiv:2311.01932v2 [math.CO] for this version)\n   | https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2311.01932 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\n\n## Submission history\n\nFrom: Péter Csikvári [view email]\n[v1] Fri, 3 Nov 2023 14:15:45 UTC (8 KB)\n[v2] Fri, 23 Feb 2024 16:08:53 UTC (9 KB)\n\nFull-text links:\n\n## Access Paper:\n\nView a PDF of the paper titled The Merino--Welsh conjecture is false for matroids, by Csongor Beke and 3 other authors\n\n  * View PDF\n  * HTML (experimental)\n  * TeX Source\n\nview license\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMerino-Welsh Conjecture (https://mathworld.wolfram.com/Merino-WelshConjecture.html)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Chen et al. (2026) determined the smallest multiplicative counterexample among parallel thickenings of uniform matroids of matroid rank Image: r on Image: n elements with Image: 0<r<n. ... \"The Merino-Welsh Conjecture Is False for Matroids.\"\n\n# Merino-Welsh Conjecture\n\nThe Merino-Welsh conjecture (Merino and Welsh 1999) asserts that every connected graph Image: G without graph loops or graph bridges satisfies\n\nImage: max{T_G(2,0),T_G(0,2)}>=T_G(1,1).   | (1)\n\nHere Image: T_G is the Tutte polynomial. The three quantities count graph orientations having no directed cycles, graph orientations in which every edge lies on a directed cycle, and spanning trees, respectively.\n\nThe multiplicative Merino-Welsh conjecture (Conde and Merino 2009) is the stronger inequality\n\nImage: T_G(2,0)T_G(0,2)>=T_G(1,1)^2.   | (2)\n\nIt remains open for graphs. Its direct extension to matroids without loops or coloops was refuted by Beke et al. (2024). A matroid loop belongs to no matroid basis, while a coloop belongs to every matroid basis.\n\nCsikvári (2026) conjectured a sharp universal replacement for matroids. Let Image: x_*=2.226681596905677... be the largest real polynomial root of\n\nImage: x^3=9(x-1).   | (3)\n\nLiu (2026) reported a proof that every finite matroid Image: M of this type satisfies\n\nImage: T_M(x_*,0)T_M(0,x_*)>=T_M(1,1)^2.   | (4)\n\nKnown counterexamples for every Image: 0<=x<x_* make Image: x_* the least nonnegative universal parameter. More precisely, if Image: M has Image: m elements and Image: kappa connected components, then for Image: 2<=x<=x_*,\n\nImage: (T_M(x,0)T_M(0,x))/(T_M(1,1)^2)>=((x^3)/(9(x-1)))^((m-2kappa)/2).   | (5)\n\nExamples in Liu (2026) show that the exponential rate is sharp throughout this interval.\n\nLiu (2026) reports that GPT-6 Astra and Claude Opus 5 assisted with mathematical exploration, proof development, exact computation, and code. As of Sep. 27, 2026, the result had not undergone external peer review, and the rational replacement table used by the proof had not been independently recomputed.\n\nChen and Xie (2026) gave a separate direct inductive proof of the universal threshold inequality Image: T_M(x_*,0)T_M(0,x_*)>=T_M(1,1)^2 for loopless and coloopless matroids. This is the same threshold already covered by Liu (2026), not a proof of the original multiplicative conjecture for graphs.\n\nFor chordal graphs, the original multiplicative inequality has been proved. Xu et al. (2026) showed that every connected bridgeless simple chordal graph satisfies Image: T_G(2,0)T_G(0,2)>=T_G(1,1)^2. Xu et al. (2026) report using ChatGPT to explore possible approaches to the proof.\n\nChen et al. (2026) determined the smallest multiplicative counterexample among parallel thickenings of uniform matroids of matroid rank Image: r on Image: n elements with Image: 0<r<n. It has 62 elements and is obtained by replacing each element of the rank-21 uniform matroid on 31 elements by two parallel copies. Every Image: q-thickening in this family with Image: q>=3 satisfies the inequality. The 62-element minimum is restricted to this family and is not asserted over all matroids or over graphs.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn minimum-size matroid counterexamples to the multiplicative Merino--Welsh inequality (https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27706)\nciteturn1academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 weeks ago; The multiplicative Merino--Welsh inequality \\[ \\Tutte_M(2,0)\\Tutte_M(0,2)\\geq \\Tutte_M(1,1)^2 \\] was proved to be false for matroids by Beke, Csáji, Csikvári, and Pituk (\\emph{Adv. ... For matroids, we further prove that every element of a minimum-size loopless and coloopless counterexample lies in exactly one series or parallel class of size two.\nTitle: On minimum-size matroid counterexamples to the multiplicative Merino--Welsh inequality\nAuthors: Jungang Chen, Xian'an Jin, Tianlong Ma\nDate: Tue Oct 20 06:23:41 2026\n\nLet $M$ be a matroid and let $\\Tutte_M(x,y)$ be its Tutte polynomial. The multiplicative Merino--Welsh inequality \\[ \\Tutte_M(2,0)\\Tutte_M(0,2)\\geq \\Tutte_M(1,1)^2 \\] was proved to be false for matroids by Beke, Csáji, Csikvári, and Pituk (\\emph{Adv. Math.} \\textbf{446} (2024), 109674). Let $U_{n,r}$ denote the rank-$r$ uniform matroid on $n$ elements, where $0<r<n$, and let $U_{n,r}^{(q)}$ denote the matroid obtained by replacing each element of $U_{n,r}$ with $q$ parallel copies. They constructed an infinite family of counterexamples of the form $U_{n,r}^{(2)}$, including $U_{33,22}^{(2)}$. In this paper, we prove that every $U_{n,r}^{(q)}$ with $q\\geq3$ satisfies the inequality. For $q=2$, we prove that the minimum number of elements in such a counterexample is $62$, attained by $U_{31,21}^{(2)}$. For loopless and bridgeless graphs, Noble and Royle (\\emph{Eur. J. Comb.} \\textbf{38} (2014), 24--35) showed that every edge of a minimum-size counterexample to the multiplicative Merino--Welsh conjecture lies in a series or parallel class of size two or three. For matroids, we further prove that every element of a minimum-size loopless and coloopless counterexample lies in exactly one series or parallel class of size two.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Merino--Welsh conjecture is false for matroids (https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.01932)\nciteturn1academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.0 years ago; Title: The Merino--Welsh conjecture is false for matroids\nTitle: The Merino--Welsh conjecture is false for matroids\nAuthors: Csongor Beke, Gergely Kál Csáji, Péter Csikvári, Sára Pituk\nDate: Fri Nov  3 14:15:45 2023\n\nThe matroidal version of the Merino--Welsh conjecture states that the Tutte polynomial $T_M(x,y)$ of any matroid $M$ without loops and coloops satisfies that $$\\max(T_M(2,0),T_M(0,2))\\geq T_M(1,1).$$ Equivalently, if the Merino--Welsh conjecture is true for all matroids without loops and coloops, then the following inequalities are also satisfied for all matroids without loops and coloops: $$T_M(2,0)+T_M(0,2)\\geq 2T_M(1,1),$$ and $$T_M(2,0)T_M(0,2)\\geq T_M(1,1)^2.$$ We show a counter-example for these inequalities.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAround the Merino\u0015Welsh conjecture (https://www.crm.cat/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/Csikvari_Peter_ClosingConference.pdf)\nciteturn1search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Around the Merino\u0015Welsh conjecture ... then the conjecture was extended to matroids.\nAround the Merino\u0015Welsh conjecture\nPéter Csikvári\nEötvös Loránd University and Alfréd Rényi Institute of Mathematics\nAbstract: In 1999 Criel Merino and Dominic Welsh proposed a conjectured\ninequality between three evaluations of the Tutte polynomial of a graph. Since\nthen the conjecture was extended to matroids. In the last two decades there have\nbeen many partial results about this conjecture. In this talk I will show a counter-\nexample to the matroidal version. I will also discuss a tool called permutation\nTutte polynomial that provided the intuition behind the counter-example and\nalso enabled us to improve on a result of Bill Jackson. This is joint work with\nCsongor Beke, Gergely Kál Csáji and Sára Pituk.\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe